              {"number": 27, "prompt": "The example of the 'million-dollar quartet' underlines the writer's point about", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "recognising talent."}, {"key": "B", "text": "working as a team."}, {"key": "C", "text": "having a shared objective."}, {"key": "D", "text": "being an effective leader."}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "Orbison, despite greater natural talent, failed because he didn't share Phillips's goal. The others succeeded because they all believed in the same vision.", "evidence": {"sectionId": "p3", "ref": "C", "quote": "Presley, Cash, Perkins and Lewis instinctively understood Phillips's ambition and believed in it. Orbison wasn't inspired by the goal"}},
              {"number": 28, "prompt": "James Watson suggests that he and Francis Crick won the race to discover the DNA code because they", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "were conscious of their own limitations."}, {"key": "B", "text": "brought complementary skills to their partnership."}, {"key": "C", "text": "were determined to outperform their brighter rivals."}, {"key": "D", "text": "encouraged each other to realise their joint ambition."}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "Watson said they succeeded 'because they were aware that they weren't the most intelligent' — conscious of their own limitations.", "evidence": {"sectionId": "p3", "ref": "F", "quote": "He said he and Crick had succeeded because they were aware that they weren't the most intelligent of the scientists pursuing the answer"}},
              {"number": 29, "prompt": "The writer mentions competitions on breakfast cereal packets as an example of how to", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "inspire creative thinking."}, {"key": "B", "text": "generate concise writing."}, {"key": "C", "text": "promote loyalty to a group."}, {"key": "D", "text": "strengthen commitment to an idea."}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "'The very act of writing makes us more likely to believe it' — writing strengthens commitment.", "evidence": {"sectionId": "p3", "ref": "H", "quote": "The very act of writing makes us more likely to believe it"}},
              {"number": 30, "prompt": "In the last paragraph, the writer suggests that it is important for employees to", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "be aware of their company's goals."}, {"key": "B", "text": "feel that their contributions are valued."}, {"key": "C", "text": "have respect for their co-workers' achievements."}, {"key": "D", "text": "understand why certain management decisions are made."}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "Cialdini says leaders should 'assure all concerned that every recommendation is important' — employees need to feel valued.", "evidence": {"sectionId": "p3", "ref": "K", "quote": "assure all concerned that every recommendation is important to making the right decision and will be given full attention"}}
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "matching",
            "instructions": "Complete each sentence with the correct ending, A–G, below. Write the correct letter, A–G.",
            "optionsLabel": "Sentence Endings",
            "options": [
              {"key": "A", "text": "take chances."},
              {"key": "B", "text": "share their ideas."},
              {"key": "C", "text": "become competitive."},
              {"key": "D", "text": "get promotion."},
              {"key": "E", "text": "avoid risk."},
              {"key": "F", "text": "ignore their duties."},
              {"key": "G", "text": "remain in their jobs."}
            ],
            "questions": [
              {"number": 31, "prompt": "Employees whose values match those of their employers are more likely to", "answer": "G", "explanation": "Value fit affects 'whether, two years after they join, they're still at the company' — they tend to remain.", "evidence": {"sectionId": "p3", "ref": "B", "quote": "the fit between an employee's values and a company's values makes a difference to what contribution they make and whether, two years after they join, they're still at the company"}},
              {"number": 32, "prompt": "At times of change, people tend to", "answer": "E", "explanation": "'When things change, we are hard-wired to play it safe' — people avoid risk.", "evidence": {"sectionId": "p3", "ref": "D", "quote": "When things change, we are hard-wired to play it safe"}},
              {"number": 33, "prompt": "If people are aware of what they might lose, they will often", "answer": "A", "explanation": "'Studies show that we invariably take more gambles when threatened with a loss' — they take chances.", "evidence": {"sectionId": "p3", "ref": "D", "quote": "we invariably take more gambles when threatened with a loss than when offered a reward"}},
              {"number": 34, "prompt": "People working under a dominant boss are liable to", "answer": "F", "explanation": "Captainitis is 'the regrettable tendency of team members to opt out of team responsibilities that are properly theirs' — they ignore their duties.", "evidence": {"sectionId": "p3", "ref": "I", "quote": "the regrettable tendency of team members to opt out of team responsibilities that are properly theirs"}},
              {"number": 35, "prompt": "Employees working in organisations with few rules are more likely to", "answer": "B", "explanation": "The Memphis collective 'encouraged a free interchange of ideas' — low-rule environments promote sharing ideas.", "evidence": {"sectionId": "p3", "ref": "J", "quote": "This environment encouraged a free interchange of ideas"}}
            ]
